6. Cross-domain collision runs

6.1 Derivative, Jacobian, and Hessian → Quorum Surface Mapper

Let interventions be (x_j) and outcomes be (y_i). The relational sensitivity matrix is

[
J_{ij}=\frac{\partial y_i}{\partial x_j}.
]

This turns vague leverage into an addressable map.

|Destination   |Inputs                                        |Outputs                                 |Use                                                  |
|--------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
|Organization  |People, policy, money, information            |Speed, quality, morale, risk            |Identify high-leverage and overloaded channels       |
|Relationship  |Time, reassurance, pressure, humor, disclosure|Trust, desire, safety, defensiveness    |Map local sensitivities without assuming universality|
|Sensory fusion|Modality-specific cues                        |Orienting, naming, affect, action       |Identify how weak signals form a quorum              |
|AI interface  |Prompt and context dimensions                 |Accuracy, novelty, stability, compliance|Locate useful steering dimensions and interactions   |

SVD of (J) reveals bundled leverage modes rather than isolated levers. A Hessian adds second-order interactions:

[
H_{jk}=\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_j\partial x_k}.
]

This exposes:

• inputs that are weak alone but strong together;
• inputs that cancel;
• saturation and reversal;
• fragile combinations;
• environmental regime changes where a lever changes sign.

The derived instrument is the Quorum Surface Mapper, directly interoperable with OQTN / Rubicon Loom.

6.2 Integral plus decay → Half-Life Ledger

Many relational states are accumulated histories rather than snapshots:

[
M(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{t}e^{-\lambda(t-\tau)}u(\tau),d\tau.
]

Here (u(\tau)) is an event stream and (\lambda) is a forgetting or recovery rate.

Possible carriers include:

• trust and betrayal;
• fatigue and recovery;
• reputation and repair;
• skill practice and decay;
• institutional precedent;
• immune or stress sensitization.

Yield: Half-Life Ledger—track not only event magnitude, but persistence, accumulation, recovery rate, and the different kernels through which agents carry history.

6.3 Convolution → Response Kernel Mapper

[
y(t)=\int x(\tau)k(t-\tau),d\tau.
]

The experienced result (y) is not produced by event (x) alone. It is the event passed through response kernel (k).

```text
same event + different memory/kernel → different experienced result
different events + similar kernel → convergent response pattern
```

Applications include relationships, markets, institutions, media audiences, immune responses, and machine interfaces.

Yield: Response Kernel Mapper—separate what happened from what the receiving system does with what happened. Intervene on the stimulus, the kernel, the timing, or their fit instead of treating response as a property of stimulus alone.

6.4 Eigenvectors → Institutional Eigenform Audit

For repeated transformation

[
x_{t+1}=Ax_t,
]

an eigenvector satisfies

[
Av=\lambda v.
]

It preserves direction while its magnitude is amplified, suppressed, or maintained.

Applied relationally:

• incentives reveal which employee behavior reproduces;
• ranking algorithms reveal which content form dominates;
• family dynamics reveal which interaction patterns persist;
• training systems reveal which capabilities they actually select;
• narratives reveal which archetypes survive retelling.

Yield: Institutional Eigenform Audit. A system’s operational objective is revealed by the pattern its repeated transformations amplify, not only by its declared mission.

6.5 Fourier plus convolution → Rhythm Separator and Resonance Engine

Moving from time basis to frequency basis can separate:

• slow trend;
• seasonal cycle;
• rapid oscillation;
• transient shock;
• noise.

Possible domains include mood, conflict, sales, attention, training, logistics, political narratives, and model performance.

Yield 1: Rhythm Separator—distinguish what recurs from what is structurally worsening.

Yield 2: Resonance Engine—combine the input spectrum with the system’s response kernel to find when a small, well-timed intervention can outperform a larger mistimed one. Timing becomes an operator, not merely a parameter.

6.6 Fixed points → Self-Reproduction Detector

A fixed point satisfies

[
x^=F(x^).
]

Examples:

• distrust produces surveillance, concealment, and renewed distrust;
• competence attracts responsibility, practice, and more competence;
• a bureaucracy produces the evidence used to justify its continuation;
• a recommendation system trains users toward what it is optimized to recommend.

Yield: Self-Reproduction Detector. If repeated repairs return to the same state, change (F)—the update rule, incentive, boundary, or feedback topology—rather than repeatedly patching the visible state.

6.7 Graph flow and cuts → Chokepoint Cartography

Max-flow asks how much can traverse a network. Min-cut asks for the smallest edge set whose removal limits that flow.

Transport domains include:

• logistics and supply chains;
• communication and attention;
• political coalitions;
• organizational authority;
• story causality;
• neural or software dependency graphs.

Yield: Chokepoint Cartography—find the few relationships that constrain a much larger flow. The same map exposes leverage and vulnerability, so ethical permissions and resilience checks must accompany its use.

6.8 PCA/SVD plus residuals → Duckling Residual Miner

Compression identifies dominant axes. Discovery often hides in what those axes fail to explain.

```text
fit dominant structure
→ preserve residual rather than discard it
→ rank by surprise × usefulness × reproducibility
→ extract mechanism or new detector
→ add detector to the next search pass
→ repeat
```

Yield: Duckling Residual Miner, the mathematical rendering of Recursive Serendipity / Nesting Cluck. Compression handles the ordinary flock; deliberate residual husbandry preserves the rare duckling that can enlarge the next detection surface.

6.9 Entropy and information gain → Golden Question Generator

For hypotheses (H) and candidate question or experiment (q), choose the action with high expected information gain:

[
q^*=\arg\max_q; \mathbb{E}\bigl[H(\text{prior})-H(\text{posterior}\mid q)\bigr].
]

Applications include debugging, diagnosis, interviewing, intelligence analysis, scientific experimentation, product discovery, and navigation.

Yield: Golden Question Generator—ask the question whose possible answers most sharply partition the live hypothesis branches, adjusted for cost, risk, and reversibility.

6.10 Symmetry across environments → Invariant Crucible

Transform the surface while observing what survives:

• change language;
• change scale;
• change medium;
• change actors;
• reverse direction;
• compress or expand;
• move from cooperative to adversarial conditions;
• move between centralized and distributed implementations.

Yield: Invariant Crucible. What survives several justified transformations is a candidate kernel. What disappears may have belonged to the original surface rather than the deep operator.

