11. Foundry protocol

1. Select an operator. Name the exact mathematical instrument rather than “math” generally.
2. Extract its anatomy. Record inputs, outputs, assumptions, branches, invariants, and failure modes.
3. Strip source surface. Replace domain nouns with typed functional roles.
4. Choose a destination domain. State why the same relational topology may exist there.
5. Choose a basis. Identify the coordinates in which the candidate relation becomes addressable.
6. Choose an environment. Specify scarcity, noise, incentives, speed, reversibility, and adaptation.
7. Cut matching teeth. Build explicit source-to-destination correspondences.
8. Set the starting pin. Declare anchors, boundary conditions, units, and initial assumptions.
9. Pull the operator. Execute quantitatively when licensed; otherwise generate a labeled heuristic prediction.
10. Inspect the seam. Record outputs, invariants, mutations, jams, missing teeth, and brittle closures.
11. Compare against a null or baseline. Test whether the operator explains or predicts more than simpler alternatives.
12. Record deltas. Preserve expected-versus-observed and source-versus-destination differences.
13. Assay other bases and environments. Separate deep structure from coordinate convenience and regime dependence.
14. Extract a new instrument. Name only what has a reconstructable operator and useful interface.
15. Promote proportionally. Mark it exact, modeled, heuristic, or decorative; update through later trials.

