    )

    native_state = encoder(observation)
    basis_state = basis.forward(native_state)
    operated = operator.apply(basis_state)
    restored = basis.inverse(operated)
    prediction = decoder(restored)

    return TrialRecord(
        prediction=prediction,
        invariants=check_invariants(signature, observation, prediction),
        reconstruction_delta=reconstruction_error(encoder, decoder, observation),
        jams=detect_jams(signature, destination, environment),
        provenance=trace_teeth(encoder, operator, decoder),
        calibration_class="unvalidated",
    )


def foundry(operator, domains, bases, environments, observations):
    trials = []
    for domain in domains:
        for basis in bases:
            for environment in environments:
                trials.append(
                    zipper_trial(
                        operator,
                        domain,
                        basis,
                        environment,
                        observations[domain],
                    )
                )

    tested = compare_to_nulls_and_reality(trials)
    return harvest_invariants_mutations_jams_and_residuals(tested)
```

14. Myth rendering — the Zipper Smith

The Smith keeps no universal key. He keeps sliders.

At each foreign gate he studies the two edges. He cuts teeth only where the materials truly correspond. He sets one pin, pulls slowly, and watches the seam. If the teeth join, two surfaces become one working enclosure. If they jam, he does not force the slider and call the damage truth. He marks the failed tooth, learns the shape of the mismatch, and recuts the instrument.

He carries every slider through heat, cold, scarcity, abundance, noise, silence, cooperation, and attack. Decorations burn away. Weak teeth split. What continues to close becomes an invariant. What fails in only one climate becomes a boundary condition. What mutates into a better fastener enters the Money Bin as a new tool.

15. Failure modes and jams

Surface analogy masquerading as structure

Two domains share vocabulary or appearance but not causal topology. Require an explicit correspondence table and at least one risky prediction.

Mathematical cosplay

Numbers or equations are attached without measurable variables, justified functional form, or calibration. Downgrade to heuristic and remove false precision.

Wrong relation family

A quadratic, linear, stationary, independent, or Gaussian model is used because it is convenient. Compare alternate families and inspect residual structure.

Basis confusion

A coordinate artifact is mistaken for an object-level truth. Repeat the analysis in another valid basis and search for invariants.

Environment leakage

An operator learned in stable, cooperative, low-noise conditions is applied in volatile or adversarial conditions without reassay.

Noninvertible translation hidden as equivalence

The decoder cannot recover important source distinctions. Record loss and avoid claiming isomorphism.

Mis-start

The initial anchor, unit, boundary, or identity is wrong, causing every later tooth to align around an error. Reinspect the starting pin before debugging downstream operations.

Forced closure

The analogy jams but the interpreter forces a neat explanation. Preserve the jam as evidence; it may reveal a missing variable or a genuine domain difference.

Overfitted seam

The mapping fits one example and splits under new instances. Require holdout examples and environmental variation.

Objective laundering

Optimization hides who chose the objective, whose constraints matter, or who bears the externalities. Keep objectives, permissions, and affected parties explicit.

Residual disposal

Compression labels all unexplained structure as noise. Preserve high-surprise residuals long enough to test whether they are measurement error, hidden variables, regime change, or Lucky Brood.

16. Validation and calibration

A transported operator graduates only when it provides at least one of the following beyond decorative analogy:

• a correct prediction;
• a useful falsifiable question;
• a measurable diagnostic;
• a better representation;
• a successful intervention under stated permissions;
• compression that preserves reconstruction;
• discovery of a boundary condition;
• repeated convergence across independent domains with plausible causal explanation.

Recommended graduation assay:

1. Test at least three destination domains.
2. Test at least two bases.
3. Test at least two contrasting environments.
4. State the null or simpler baseline.
5. Preserve failed trials and negative results.
6. Separate fit, prediction, interpretation, and counterfactual.
7. Record confidence and calibration class.
8. Re-test after the target adapts, when applicable.

17. Ethical robustness

MAT may support design, diagnosis, explanation, education, coordination, and decision support. It must not use mathematical language to conceal coercion, manufacture certainty, or optimize people toward thresholds they have not consented to cross.

Required safeguards:

• make objective functions and constraints inspectable;
• distinguish a model of a person from the person’s agency;
• require stronger evidence for high-stakes or irreversible action;
• prefer reversible probes during early calibration;
• disclose major losses introduced by encoding or compression;
• do not treat inferred sensitivities as permission to exploit them;
• preserve provenance, dissenting branches, and repair history;
• distinguish descriptive equilibria from desirable ones;
• measure externalities and distribution of benefit, not only aggregate performance.

